    "result_text": "The full suite is green \u2014 there are only 3 test files (`AcceptanceBase` is abstract, `AuditLogCapture` is a helper), so `AcceptanceTest` is the only test class.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe spec: reject owner creation with **429** once 100+ owners have been registered today (by `registrationDate`).\n\nTo keep structural impact low, I mirrored the existing pattern in the `addOwner` flow (`CityCodes`, `Households`, `Namesakes` are all small standalone counter classes) rather than growing the large `OwnerRestControllerV1` class:\n\n1. **New file** `DailyRegistrations.java` \u2014 a tiny cohesive helper (WMC_other \u2248 0) that counts owners whose `registrationDate` equals a given day.\n\n2. **`OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`** \u2014 added a 3-line guard right after the owners are already fetched (reusing the existing `findAllOwners()` call, so no extra query and no new field/import beyond a fully-qualified `LocalDate`):\n\n```java\nif (DailyRegistrations.countOn(owners, java.time.LocalDate.now()) >= 100) {\n    return new ResponseEntity<>(HttpStatus.TOO_MANY_REQUESTS);\n}\n```\n\nThe check keys off `registrationDate`, which is defaulted to the current date via the entity's `@PrePersist`, so the 100 owners created today are counted and the 101st create returns 429.",
